## The problem

A task board is a shared mutable document. The moment two people use it at once,
you inherit every distributed systems problem in miniature: two managers reassign
the same task within the same second, someone drags a card while offline, a
socket drops mid-update and reconnects with stale state.

Getting a WebSocket to deliver a message is straightforward. Deciding what the
board should look like when messages arrive out of order, twice, or not at all is
the actual work — and it is where naive implementations quietly corrupt state.

## Architecture

**Rooms scoped to boards.** Each board is a Socket.io room. Clients join on open
and leave on close, so an update fans out to exactly the people looking at that
board rather than to everyone connected. Room membership is authorised on join,
not on emit — checking permission once at the boundary rather than on every
message.

**The server is the only writer.** Clients send intents (`task.move`,
`task.assign`), never state. The server validates the intent against current
state and the actor's role, applies it, and broadcasts the result. Clients render
what the server confirms.

```js
socket.on("task.move", async (intent, ack) => {
  const { boardId, taskId, toColumn, expectedVersion } = intent;

  if (!can(socket.user, "task:move", boardId)) {
    return ack({ ok: false, error: "forbidden" });
  }

  const task = await Task.findOne({ _id: taskId, boardId });
  if (!task) return ack({ ok: false, error: "not_found" });

  // Optimistic concurrency: a stale client loses and is told why, rather
  // than silently overwriting someone else's move.
  if (task.version !== expectedVersion) {
    return ack({ ok: false, error: "conflict", current: task });
  }

  task.column = toColumn;
  task.version += 1;
  await task.save();

  // Everyone else in the room, including the sender's other tabs.
  socket.to(boardId).emit("task.moved", task);
  ack({ ok: true, task });
});
```

The `expectedVersion` check is what makes this safe. Without it, last-write-wins
means the slower network connection silently overwrites a decision someone else
already made. With it, the losing client is told there was a conflict and shown
the current state — which is recoverable, and honest.

**Optimistic UI with reconciliation.** The client applies a move immediately so
dragging feels instant, and holds the previous state until the server
acknowledges. On conflict it rolls back and re-renders from the server's
version. Fast in the common case, correct in the uncommon one.

**Reconnection replays rather than reloads.** A dropped socket resumes with the
last event id it saw; the server sends everything since. Falls back to a full
board fetch if the gap is too large to replay.

## Challenges

**Duplicate delivery.** Reconnects meant clients occasionally processed the same
event twice. Making every event carry an id and having clients ignore ids they
have already applied was a smaller fix than the debugging that led to it.

**Permissions drifting from rooms.** Early on, a user removed from a board mid-
session kept receiving updates because they were still in the room. Revoking
access now force-leaves the room, rather than relying on the next join check.

**The admin dashboard.** Live progress across all teams, naively implemented,
meant subscribing to every board — which does not scale past a handful of users.
Replaced with an aggregated summary channel emitting periodic rollups instead of
raw per-task events.

## Outcome

A working collaborative platform with real-time sync, role-based access control,
and an admin view of live progress across teams.

What I took from it: real-time features are usually presented as a transport
problem and are almost always a consistency problem. The socket is the easy part.
Deciding what happens when two truths arrive at once is the design, and it is
worth doing before writing the first `emit`.
